// An entity-component-system: components in parallel arrays keyed by entity.
//
// An ECS normally exists because game state is a large mutable graph and every
// system wants a different slice. Blot has no mutation, so that framing does
// not apply — but the layout still does, and it is the layout that is reusable.
// Nothing here knows what a component is, so a store holds whatever the
// application decided its entities have.
//
// This module needs no authority. A store is an array and a system is a
// function over arrays, so the module input is unit and the importer's
// effect row is unchanged by using it.

open import "blot:prelude"

// One slot per entity. The slot is an `Option` because a join has to answer
// what happens where a component is absent, and `#None` is that answer — but
// nothing here hands one out: `attach` is the only way to grow a store, and it
// always fills. A store becomes sparse when something detaches, which this
// library does not do yet, or when two stores are grown to different lengths.
//
// An entity id indexes every store alike, which is what makes a join an index
// walk rather than a search.
const Store = fn component => [Option component]

const attach = fn (store, value) => @array.push store (Some value)
const at = fn (store, id) => case Array.get (store, id) of
  #Some value => value
  #None => None
const replace = fn (store, id, value) => case Array.set (store, id, value) of
  #Some updated => updated
  #None => store
const count = fn store => Array.length store

// A system over one component: rebuild the store from the entities that have
// it. The entities that do not are carried through untouched, so a system is a
// total function over the world even when it reads a slice of it.
const each_1 = fn (store, step) => do:
  let state = store
  for id in Iter.range (0, count store):
    state := case at (store, id) of
      #Some value => replace (state, id, Some (step value))
      #None => state

  return state

// The join a two-component system wants: rebuild the left store for entities
// that have both components and skip the rest.
//
// The pair is the scrutinee, so the entity that has both components is one arm
// and everything else is the other. Reading both stores before either is
// tested is what "an entity id indexes every store alike" already promised.
const each_2 = fn (left, right, step) => do:
  let state = left
  for id in Iter.range (0, count left):
    state := case (at (left, id), at (right, id)) of
      (#Some a, #Some b) => replace (state, id, Some (step (a, b)))
      _ => state

  return state

// The same join for a system that produces effects rather than components,
// which is what a renderer is.
//
// The nesting here is not a preference, and it is no longer the backend
// refusing the shape. Written as `case (at (left, id), at (right, id)) of`, the
// pair is what carries the component out — and inference then records, at the
// visitor's own `transform.position`, the three fields that visitor projects
// rather than the four the store holds. The backend builds the record's runtime
// type from that set and refuses the mismatch. Until a shape survives
// a tuple scrutinee, a renderer's join stays two `case`s.
const visit_2 = fn (left, right, visit) => do:
  for id in Iter.range (0, count left):
    <- case at (left, id) of
      #Some a => case at (right, id) of
        #Some b => visit (a, b)
        #None => ()

      #None => ()

  return ()

return {
  .Store = Store;
  .attach = attach;
  .at = at;
  .count = count;
  .each_1 = each_1;
  .each_2 = each_2;
  .visit_2 = visit_2;
}
